<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: title, description and keywords in Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the deck’s title, description and keywords, translated by an AI agent from the English (the Swedish settling what was ambiguous) into Korean, German, Spanish and French, in the words the app itself uses, and checked by a second AI agent that translated every text back into English and fixed what had drifted; the release compared by a script with version 1, which found the new title, description and keywords and the release’s own metadata the only changes, and checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the title, description and keywords are in Korean, German, Spanish and French as well; no card changed."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 3: backs in Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every card’s back, its title, painter and date, in Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French: titles and painters from each painting’s and painter’s Wikidata labels in that language where they were right, checked by AI agents against the English back and the language’s Wikipedia, and otherwise the title or name that language’s Wikipedia uses (111 of the 750 new titles, mostly where Wikidata had no label or a vandalised or less used one); dates written in each language from the Wikidata date and its precision; the release compared by a script with version 2, which found the new backs, the description, the languages and the release’s own metadata the only changes, and checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: every one of the 150 backs is in Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French as well; one English back was corrected: A Philosopher Lecturing on the Orrery, which version 2 had as “A Journalist Lecturing on the Orrery” from a vandalised Wikidata label (every other language’s label and the painting’s Wikipedia articles say philosopher); no card was added or removed."@en .

